{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "category_ticket_volume",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Ticket count per category is a genuine, directly relevant axis for identifying which ticket types create the heaviest workload. Values are derivable by aggregating category across rows, consistent with the sample values."
    },
    {
      "name": "category_avg_resolution_hours",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.15,
      "source_groundedness": 0.2,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Conceptually central to the query, but the column is degenerate: every value is 0.0. The source rows have opened_at available (not shown) and closed_at, so a real resolution time is computable, yet all values collapse to 0, indicating a broken/constant computation that misrepresents the data. Near-constant and unsupported by evidence."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_heavy_workload_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A heavy-workload flag derived from volume gives a clean grouping variable for the query. Somewhat redundant with category_ticket_volume but adds a usable threshold categorisation. Flags align with the volume samples (high-volume categories flagged 1, lower flagged 0)."
    },
    {
      "name": "estimated_resolution_time_hours",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.05,
      "source_groundedness": 0.3,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "All values are NULL across 500 rows, providing no analytical leverage. Not affirmatively hallucinated (NULL is not a false assertion), but as a fully empty column it fails to help the resolution-time analysis it was meant to support."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.45,
  "source_groundedness": 0.55,
  "rationale": "The volume and heavy-workload columns are well-targeted and grounded, giving real axes to identify the workload-heavy ticket types. However, the two columns that speak directly to resolution time both fail: category_avg_resolution_hours is uniformly 0.0 (broken/misleading given closed_at and opening timestamps exist) and estimated_resolution_time_hours is entirely NULL. Since the query hinges on quantifying resolution-time impact, the operator delivers the grouping side but not the outcome side."
}

